  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contract obligations in Maine totaled $2.8B in FY2026 to date. The federal government has issued 7,373 awards in the period, with an average award size of $381K. Obligations fell 28.6% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $2.7B of the state total so far in FY2026. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry sector by obligation, at $2B, and BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION is the largest vendor, receiving $2B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the second-largest industry, at $433M, and MARTINS POINT HEALTH CARE INC is the second-largest vendor, at $433M. Historical obligations show a peak of $3.9B in FY25, with FY24 and FY23 each at $3.7B, FY22 at $2.7B, and FY21 at $2.4B. All figures are contract obligations and the FY2026 totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How does current federal spending in Maine compare to prior fiscal years?",
        "body": "FY2026 to date obligations are $2.8B, below the FY25 total of $3.9B. Obligations were $3.7B in both FY24 and FY23, $2.7B in FY22, and $2.4B in FY21. Obligations fell 28.6% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which buyers, industries, and vendors dominate Maine federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $2.7B in FY2026 to date, more than any other agency. Ship Building and Repairing is the largest industry at $2B, and BATH IRON WORKS CORPORATION is the largest vendor at $2B. Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers and MARTINS POINT HEALTH CARE INC follow with $433M each."
      }
    ]
  },
